Research Quality Assurance and Report Accuracy

1. Introduction

Quality assurance is an important part of the research process because it ensures that the information, experimental procedures, measurements and conclusions presented in the study are accurate, consistent and reliable.

This project evaluates FIFO, Priority Queuing (PQ), and Weighted Fair Queuing (WFQ) under simulated bandwidth-constrained network conditions. Quality assurance will therefore focus on verifying the technical information, experimental configurations, performance measurements and research findings.

2. Technical Information Verification

The technical descriptions of the three scheduling algorithms will be reviewed to ensure that they correctly explain their operating principles.

The verification process will examine:

- FIFO packet ordering and queue management.
- PQ traffic classification and priority-based scheduling.
- WFQ flow classification and weighted bandwidth allocation.

Information will be checked against credible networking textbooks, academic publications and technical documentation.

3. Dataset Verification

The MAWI Working Group Traffic Archive will be checked to confirm that:

- The selected traffic trace exists and can be accessed.
- The correct trace filename and date are documented.
- The dataset source is properly acknowledged.
- Applicable usage and licensing conditions are respected.
- The limitations of using MAWI traffic as a proxy for rural Zimbabwean network traffic are clearly explained.

Dataset source: https://mawi.wide.ad.jp/mawi/

4. Performance Measurement Verification

The performance evaluation will use four metrics:

Throughput: Verify that successfully received data is measured over the correct time interval.

Packet Delay: Check that packet transmission and reception timestamps are used consistently when calculating average delay.

Packet Loss: Verify that transmitted and received packet counts are correctly recorded.

Flow Fairness: Check the calculation and interpretation of Jain's Fairness Index.

All calculations will be checked before the results are included in the final report.

5. Experimental Consistency

To maintain a fair comparison, the following conditions must remain consistent across the three scheduling algorithms:

- Traffic inputs.
- Available bandwidth.
- Queue capacity.
- Experimental duration.
- Measurement procedures.

Any configuration differences must be documented and justified.

6. Results and Documentation Review

The results will be reviewed to ensure that:

- Tables accurately represent measured data.
- Graphs use correctly labelled axes and units.
- Calculations agree with recorded experimental results.
- Interpretations are supported by evidence.
- References are correctly cited.
- Conclusions do not make claims beyond what the experiments demonstrate.

7. Limitations and Research Integrity

The study uses MAWI traffic traces as proxy traffic in a simulated constrained-bandwidth environment. Therefore, findings will be limited to the selected simulation conditions and will not be presented as direct measurements of actual rural Zimbabwean ISP performance.

No experimental results will be invented or altered to favour a particular scheduling algorithm.

8. Expected Outcome

The quality assurance process is expected to improve the accuracy, reliability, consistency and academic credibility of the final research report and presentation.